This uses the same cancellation idea without storing a count for every number. The temporary map can hold at most two values. When a third value arrives, all three different values lose one count and entries that reach zero are removed.

The remaining values are only possible answers, so I count them again before returning anything. That final check matters because a surviving candidate does not always pass the one-third limit.

```python
class Solution:
    def majorityElementTwo(self, nums):
        candidates = {}

        for value in nums:
            if value in candidates:
                candidates[value] += 1
            elif len(candidates) < 2:
                candidates[value] = 1
            else:
                for candidate in list(candidates):
                    candidates[candidate] -= 1
                    if candidates[candidate] == 0:
                        del candidates[candidate]

        frequencies = {value: 0 for value in candidates}
        for value in nums:
            if value in frequencies:
                frequencies[value] += 1

        threshold = len(nums) // 3
        return [value for value, count in frequencies.items()
                if count > threshold]
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the candidate map has at most two entries.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, since the candidate and frequency maps never hold more than two keys. The input list is not changed. On an empty input, both maps stay empty and the returned list is empty.

A frequency map is the most direct way to handle this. Count every number first, then keep the ones whose count is greater than `n / 3`. There can be at most two values in the result, but the map makes the rule easy to apply.

For example, with `nums = [1, 2, 1, 3, 1]`, the counts are `1 -> 3`, `2 -> 1`, and `3 -> 1`. Since `5 / 3` is `1`, only `1` is returned.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> majorityElementTwo(int[] nums) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        int threshold = nums.length / 3;

        for (int value : nums) {
            counts.put(value, counts.getOrDefault(value, 0) + 1);
        }

        for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : counts.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() > threshold) {
                result.add(entry.getKey());
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the map. The input array is not changed.

Sorting puts equal values next to each other, so a single scan can count each group. When the value changes, check the group that just ended. The final group needs a separate check after the loop.

This version changes `nums` because it calls `sort()`. That is fine when the input order is not needed later.

```python
class Solution:
    def majorityElementTwo(self, nums):
        if not nums:
            return []

        nums.sort()
        result = []
        threshold = len(nums) // 3
        current_count = 1

        for index in range(1, len(nums)):
            if nums[index] == nums[index - 1]:
                current_count += 1
            else:
                if current_count > threshold:
                    result.append(nums[index - 1])
                current_count = 1

        if current_count > threshold:
            result.append(nums[-1])

        return result
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because of sorting, followed by an `O(n)` scan.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space apart from the sorting implementation. The input list is modified. An empty list returns an empty result.

Only two numbers can appear more than one third of the time. I keep two candidate slots and cancel one occurrence from both slots whenever a third different value appears. The first pass only finds possible candidates, so the second pass checks their real frequencies.

For a quick dry run on `[1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 1, 2]`, the candidates settle on `1` and `2`. Their final counts are both `3`, while `7 / 3` is `2`, so both are returned.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> majorityElementTwo(vector<int>& nums) {
        int candidate1 = 0;
        int candidate2 = 0;
        int vote1 = 0;
        int vote2 = 0;

        for (int value : nums) {
            if (value == candidate1) {
                vote1++;
            } else if (value == candidate2) {
                vote2++;
            } else if (vote1 == 0) {
                candidate1 = value;
                vote1 = 1;
            } else if (vote2 == 0) {
                candidate2 = value;
                vote2 = 1;
            } else {
                vote1--;
                vote2--;
            }
        }

        int frequency1 = 0;
        int frequency2 = 0;
        for (int value : nums) {
            if (value == candidate1) {
                frequency1++;
            } else if (value == candidate2) {
                frequency2++;
            }
        }

        vector<int> result;
        int threshold = nums.size() / 3;

        if (frequency1 > threshold) {
            result.push_back(candidate1);
        }
        if (frequency2 > threshold) {
            result.push_back(candidate2);
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` from the candidate pass and verification pass.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, not counting the returned list. The input array stays unchanged.

Kept this version close to the definition. For every different number, count how many times it appears by scanning the full array. A set prevents checking the same value more than once, and another set keeps the result free of duplicates.

Dry run for `[3, 2, 3]`: the count for `3` is `2`, and `n / 3` is `1`, so `3` is added. The count for `2` is only `1`, so it is skipped.

```cpp
#include <unordered_set>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> majorityElementTwo(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> result;
        unordered_set<int> checked;
        int threshold = nums.size() / 3;

        for (int value : nums) {
            if (checked.count(value) != 0) {
                continue;
            }

            checked.insert(value);
            int frequency = 0;

            for (int other : nums) {
                if (other == value) {
                    frequency++;
                }
            }

            if (frequency > threshold) {
                result.push_back(value);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case, since each distinct value may scan the array.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the set of checked values. The input array is not changed. For an array with one value, that value is returned because its count is greater than zero.

